-- Production test with a noisy meter: guard bands and retests
--
-- A factory tests every 3.3 V regulator it builds and ships the ones whose output is within
-- ±30 mV of nominal. The meter has errors of its own, so some good parts measure out of spec and
-- are scrapped (false rejects), and some bad parts measure in spec and are shipped (false accepts).
-- The usual remedy is a guard band: accept only readings comfortably inside the limits.
--
-- Assumptions, labeled as such:
--   * Production spread: the true output error is bell-shaped with a standard deviation of 15 mV,
--     written as a binomial in 2 mV steps. About 4.5% of parts are out of spec.
--   * The meter's error is bell-shaped and bounded, the way a meter's accuracy spec is. How large
--     it is is only roughly known: a standard deviation of about 2.8 mV with errors of at most
--     ±8 mV, or possibly 4.2 mV and ±12 mV. That makes `unit`, the size of the meter's error
--     steps, a fuzzy parameter.
--   * Each reading of the same part has an independent meter error.

param unit : Num ~= {2: 1, 3: 0.5}

input k : Num ~ binomial(225, 0.5)
def error_mv : Num := (k - 112) * 2 - 1     -- the part's true output minus 3.3 V, in mV (odd values)
def in_spec : Bool := abs(error_mv) <= 30

input r1 : Num ~ binomial(8, 0.5)
input r2 : Num ~ binomial(8, 0.5)
input r3 : Num ~ binomial(8, 0.5)

-- whether a reading with meter noise r lies within the limits, narrowed by a guard band in mV
def passes(r : Num, guard : Num) : Bool := abs(error_mv + (r - 4) * unit) <= 30 - guard

#prob not in_spec

-- 1. One reading at the spec limits. The false-accept rate, under 1%, meets the 2% that the
-- ANSI/NCSL Z540.3 standard allows for calibration. But about 0.7% of the parts shipped are out
-- of spec, 6,500 parts per million or more, far above a typical target of 1,000. Haze shows one
-- that gets through: a part 37 mV low that reads 29 mV low.
claim false_accept_z540 : passes(r1, 0) and not in_spec with prob <= 0.02
#prob in_spec and not passes(r1, 0)                                  -- good parts scrapped
claim shipped_under_1000_ppm : not in_spec given passes(r1, 0) with prob <= 0.001

-- 2. Retesting a failed part until it passes, up to three readings, roughly doubles the share
-- of bad parts shipped: an out-of-spec part only needs one lucky reading.
def retest_pass : Bool := passes(r1, 0) or passes(r2, 0) or passes(r3, 0)
claim retest_under_1000_ppm : not in_spec given retest_pass with prob <= 0.001

-- 3. Guard bands. A 6 mV band meets the 1,000 ppm target with either meter and scraps about 7%
-- of good parts. A band as wide as the meter's largest error makes shipping a bad part
-- impossible: 8 mV is enough for the better meter but not the noisier one, and 12 mV is enough
-- for both, at the price of scrapping about 19% of good parts.
claim guard_6_under_1000_ppm : not in_spec given passes(r1, 6) with prob <= 0.001
#prob in_spec and not passes(r1, 6)
theorem guard_8_ships_only_good : passes(r1, 8) implies in_spec
theorem guard_12_ships_only_good : passes(r1, 12) implies in_spec
#prob in_spec and not passes(r1, 12)
